            <ArticleTitle>Lumbar radiculopathy caused by a tunneling transvertebral Schmorl's node.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>484-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We report a rare case of lumbar spinal radiculopathy caused by a tunneling Schmorl's node originating from the superior endplate of the L3 vertebra of a 75-year-old patient and penetrating the spinal canal through the posterior wall of this vertebra. This case highlights recent reports emphasizing the clinical pain syndromes possibly associated with Schmorl's nodes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
